[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/5996328687","html_url":"https://github.com/google/agentmesh/issues/561#issuecomment-5996328687","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/561","id":5996328687,"node_id":"IC_kwDOSIvpys8AAAABZWi27w","user":{"login":"aojea","id":6450081,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY0NTAwODE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6450081?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea","html_url":"https://github.com/aojea","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aojea/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-05T14:17:35Z","updated_at":"2026-10-05T14:18:10Z","body":"I think we need to evolve from an \"ambient\" / transport oriented mesh to a more \"task oriented mesh\", that means we need to perform a change on the architecture for implementing the right security posture ... agents requirements require more granular authorization as described here and that requires some fundamental architectural changes I'm preparing\n","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/5996328687/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6087845489","html_url":"https://github.com/google/agentmesh/issues/561#issuecomment-6087845489","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/561","id":6087845489,"node_id":"IC_kwDOSIvpys8AAAABat0mcQ","user":{"login":"Paraphern","id":297951147,"node_id":"U_kgDOEcJfqw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/297951147?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern","html_url":"https://github.com/Paraphern","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-09T19:31:07Z","updated_at":"2026-10-09T19:31:07Z","body":"We built this exact mechanism as a standalone tool (pin tool contracts at approval, diff on every update, fail the build on mismatch) and ran it as a field audit across the most-installed MCP servers on npm. Your three open questions all have field-tested answers now:\n\n**On canonicalisation (your hardest question).** We hash each tool three ways: full contract, canonicalized schema, prose (description text). The canonicalization sorts object keys (array order stays significant — otherwise the first differing byte can mask a changed enum) but deliberately does *not* normalize the declared `$schema` dialect or the `additionalProperties` form. A live case explains why: chrome-devtools-mcp 1.8.0→1.10.1 (MCP SDK v2 migration) re-serialized all 28 tool schemas — dialect declaration changed from draft-07 to 2020-12, `additionalProperties` went from `true` to `{}`, and every key reordered. A naive digest would flag all 28 as changed on a day the vendor shipped zero parameter edits. With key-order canonicalization alone, all 28 still flag; only when you additionally strip `$schema` and normalize empty `additionalProperties` does the diff collapse to the true residue: 0 parameter-level changes (SDK-generated integer bounds `±9007199254740991` on `timeout`/`pageSize`, description text edits, one loosened `additionalProperties: false` removal). We keep dialect changes visible because validators treat them as contract — but that's the trade-off: normalize too little and you train \"ignore the alarm\", normalize too much and you risk swallowing a real change.\n\n**On opt-in vs default.** Our field data settles this: across 9 top npm MCP servers (windows from 5 weeks to 6 months), 4 drifted and 5 were clean — Notion went 3 months, Heroku shipped 6 patches, context7 held through 3 minor versions, all zero-drift. This is not \"servers change constantly\"; it's a few fast movers (azure-devops: 110 findings in 6 months of minors; currents: 45 in 3 months). A mesh-wide default would generate approval fatigue concentrated on the wrong servers. Opt-in per caller is right, exactly as you sketched.\n\n**On where the first digest comes from.** Trust-on-first-use is the only practical bootstrap for a mesh where callers discover providers dynamically. Our experience: TOFU + an explicit approve step after first connection. The pin is recorded automatically on first successful `tools/list`, but the caller (or operator) marks it as reviewed — and only that mark makes the digest authoritative for later sessions. Un-reviewed pins still diff (so you see the change), but the session continues; reviewed pins hard-fail. This matches your `LabelsNotSatisfiedError` shape: one typed error for \"un-reviewed change\" (informational), one for \"reviewed contract violated\" (fatal).\n\nOne thing your sketch doesn't cover yet, and our field data says it matters: **not all changes are equal**. We grade drift three ways — BREAKING (parameters added/removed/type-changed, constraints added), LOOSENED (constraints dropped, types widened), NOTATION (semantically void: dialect, key order, `additionalProperties` form). All three exit non-zero (it's still drift), but the reviewer reads substance instead of noise. The chrome migration above scores 0 BREAKING + 30 NOTATION with grading — without it, 28 BREAKING flags on a nothing-burger train people to click through.\n\nTest material: our repo ships a public corpus of benign/weaponized MCP pairs (`corpus/`), including a sleeper that flips its tool description after N calls (the Deadbugz shape) and a key-reorder-only pair (canonicalization regression test). stdio, zero deps, take freely: https://github.com/Paraphern/rugsnare\n\n(For prior-art context: the standalone tool is our working implementation of this mechanism — per-tool three-hash pinning, change classification, approve verb, CI gate, live proxy. The field data above is its output. The approach you sketched — session-scoped `expected_tools_digest` alongside `required_labels` — is architecturally cleaner than our external tool for a mesh context; the open questions above are where the implementation details live.)\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6087845489/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6395612661","html_url":"https://github.com/google/agentmesh/issues/561#issuecomment-6395612661","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/561","id":6395612661,"node_id":"IC_kwDOSIvpys8AAAABfTVN9Q","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-25T08:51:56Z","updated_at":"2026-10-25T08:51:56Z","body":"Data on the three open questions from a public record of the tool lists of 38,126 MCP servers (registry remotes plus npm/PyPI packages). It complements Paraphern's per-server windows above. We run that record (HISTOR), so read it with that in mind.\n\n**Opt-in or default.** From 24 September to 20 October we recorded 23,821 tool-list changes on 9,472 servers. 5,862 of those servers changed exactly once, and the 100 busiest account for 8% of all changes. Of the remote servers we had pinned by 24 September, 45% (4,926 of 10,929) served a different tool list by 20 October. At registry scale, drift is spread thin rather than held by a few fast movers, so a mesh-wide default would ask for re-approval on almost any long-lived set of providers. A caller-supplied pin, as you sketched, fits that.\n\n**Canonicalisation.** The profile we use (MTL/1) digests four members per tool: `name`, `description`, `inputSchema`, `outputSchema`. It sorts tools by name in UTF-16 code-unit order, not locale order (`\"Z_tool\".localeCompare(\"a_tool\")` is 1, code-unit order gives -1). It serialises with RFC 8785 and hashes with SHA-256. RFC 8785 fixes key order and integer formatting. For a tool set with a fractional number anywhere in its schemas, the profile gives no digest and reports the set as inconclusive, because two implementations may print the same float differently. A missing digest is better than one nobody else can reproduce. `title`, `annotations` and `_meta` are outside this digest. Since 22 October we record them under a separate digest, so a tool that drops `readOnlyHint: true` shows up as its own change without moving the tool-set pin.\n\n**Where the first digest comes from.** Besides trust on first use and operator configuration, there is a third source: an independent observer. HISTOR reconnects to listed servers on a schedule, issues a signed label carrying each tool-set digest, and appends the label to an RFC 9162 Merkle log with signed tree heads. On first use, a caller can compare the digest it received with the one the log recorded (`GET https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/servers/<id>`, field `toolSetDigest`), and keep the inclusion proof (`GET /api/v1/labels/<label id>/proof`) as evidence of what was approved.\n\nProfile: https://github.com/alexar76/aicom/blob/main/awr/adoption/mcp-trust-label/PROFILE.md · data (CC BY 4.0): https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512760\n\nAI disclosure: drafted with an AI assistant from our data. The counts were recomputed from the published files.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6395612661/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6396447622","html_url":"https://github.com/google/agentmesh/issues/561#issuecomment-6396447622","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/561","id":6396447622,"node_id":"IC_kwDOSIvpys8AAAABfUILhg","user":{"login":"Paraphern","id":297951147,"node_id":"U_kgDOEcJfqw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/297951147?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern","html_url":"https://github.com/Paraphern","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Paraphern/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-25T10:21:59Z","updated_at":"2026-10-25T10:21:59Z","body":"The 45% figure lands harder than the per-server windows we posted — a month is enough for nearly half of any long-lived pinned set to have served something different, which settles opt-in-vs-default on its own.\n\nOn canonicalisation: having just run a cross-vendor digest pair (our `surfaceHash` vs Dominion Observatory's `manifest_hash` over one public endpoint — identical bytes, [rugsnare#6](https://github.com/Paraphern/rugsnare/issues/6)), the MTL/1 profile is a third flavor of the same base (RFC 8785 + SHA-256), and the deltas are exactly the ones a spec-level answer should force every profile to *state* rather than leave to implementation accident:\n\n1. **Tools sort unit.** MTL/1 sorts by UTF-16 code units; PIN-SPEC §3.2 by UTF-8 bytes (code-point order). Identical for ASCII names; divergent for supplementary-plane characters — a high surrogate sorts *below* U+E000–U+FFFF names in code-unit order and *above* them in code-point order. Two correct implementations that disagree only on the unit will produce different digests for the same tool set, and every consumer will read that as \"someone changed something.\" (Dominion's published profile says \"sorted by name\" without naming the unit — that question is simply open there, and our byte-order sort reproducing their live capture doesn't exercise it: the names in that capture are ASCII.)\n2. **Field set.** 3 fields (PIN-SPEC, Dominion) vs 4 (MTL/1 adds `outputSchema`). Fine while profiles are named — a join that doesn't state the set isn't a join.\n3. **Floats.** Three honest answers now exist: specified (JCS §3.2.2.3, ours), unexercised (Dominion — no non-integer in any captured schema), refused (MTL/1 — no digest, set reported inconclusive). \"Refused\" is the most conservative and worth respecting; whatever a standard picks, the policy should be mandatory to declare.\n\nOne convergence worth flagging for the design: MTL/1's separate annotation digest (since 2026-10-22) is the same decision as our ANNOTATION finding class — annotations outside the tool-set digest, surfaced as their own change. Two independent implementations landing on the same split is evidence the split is right.\n\nWe wrote the full three-way comparison up (with the live pair as the worked example): [comparison-dominion-captures.md](https://github.com/Paraphern/rugsnare/blob/main/audits/drift-watch/comparison-dominion-captures.md). And one practical note for MTL/1 §4.6: the consumer-recompute requirement is hard for stdio subjects (recompute = execute), but a public HTTPS endpoint is recomputable without running anything — the hosted endpoint behind that pair is available as a reference point for anyone testing label verification.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/google/agentmesh/issues/comments/6396447622/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]